.TH "true-knowledge" "1" "Oct 2026" "true-knowledge" "tk (true-knowledge)"

.SH NAME
tk-completion-zsh - Generate the autocompletion script for zsh


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtk completion zsh [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Generate the autocompletion script for the zsh shell.

.PP
If shell completion is not already enabled in your environment you will need
to enable it.  You can execute the following once:

.EX
echo "autoload -U compinit; compinit" >> ~/.zshrc
.EE

.PP
To load completions in your current shell session:

.EX
source <(tk completion zsh)
.EE

.PP
To load completions for every new session, execute once:

.SS Linux:
.EX
tk completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_tk"
.EE

.SS macOS:
.EX
tk completion zsh > $(brew --prefix)/share/zsh/site-functions/_tk
.EE

.PP
You will need to start a new shell for this setup to take effect.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for zsh

.PP
\fB--no-descriptions\fP[=false]
	disable completion descriptions


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--budget\fP=0
	char budget override (0 = config defaults)

.PP
\fB--home\fP=""
	override base home (TK_HOME equivalent)

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	stable JSON envelope output

.PP
\fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	errors only on stderr; suppress progress

.PP
\fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	debug narration on stderr


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtk-completion(1)\fP
